-- =============================================================================
-- 14. GRANTS
-- =============================================================================
-- The anonymous role (used by the Flutter web app via the anon key) has full
-- DML access to all public tables. The authenticated and service_role roles
-- have the same access. This is the default Supabase pattern: access control
-- is managed at the application level (RLS), not at the grant level.
--
-- Current RLS status: NO tables have RLS enabled (see section 15 for the
-- deferred audit_log hard-lock). All access is governed by the GRANTs alone.

-- All four roles get full DML on every table. The pattern is:
--   GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, TRUNCATE, REFERENCES, TRIGGER
--   ON <table> TO <role>;
-- Applied to: anon, authenticated, postgres, service_role
-- Applied to: artists, records, genres, descriptors, record_artists,
--   record_genres, record_descriptors, record_streaming, genre_hierarchy,
--   descriptor_hierarchy, audit_log


-- =============================================================================
-- 15. RLS NOTES  (audit_log hard-lock, DEFERRED)
-- =============================================================================
-- The audit_log table should eventually be locked to INSERT-only (no client
-- can UPDATE or DELETE rows). This is achieved by:
--
--   ALTER TABLE audit_log ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
--
--   -- Block all SELECTs (logs are never read via the DB; they're read via
--   -- PostgREST/anon, and the app never needs to SELECT from audit_log).
--   CREATE POLICY audit_log_block_all ON audit_log
--     FOR SELECT USING (false);
--
--   -- Allow INSERTs only (the SECURITY DEFINER trigger bypasses RLS, and
--   -- the client's app_boot insert also needs INSERT).
--   CREATE POLICY audit_log_insert_anon ON audit_log
--     FOR INSERT WITH CHECK (true);
--
--   -- No UPDATE policy -> denied for all non-owner roles.
--   -- No DELETE policy -> denied for all non-owner roles.
--
-- This is deliberately DEFERRED until the audit log has been verified correct
-- via the app's verify_audit_log probe. The SQL for this lives in:
--   phase 6 deployment/temp rls/supabase_migration_audit_log_rls.sql
--
-- Why SECURITY DEFINER still works after RLS is enabled:
--   fn_audit_log() is SECURITY DEFINER, meaning it runs as the function owner
--   (postgres), not as the calling role. The owner bypasses RLS entirely, so
--   the trigger can still write log rows even after RLS is turned on. Only
--   direct client UPDATE/DELETE is blocked.
-- =============================================================================
